INSERT INTO mentor_knowledge (slug, title, lesson_id, lang, sort, content)
VALUES ('what-is-trading-en', $kb$What Is Trading? (lesson 01)$kb$, 'l1', 'en', 100, $kb$Transcript of the academy lesson video: What Is Trading? (lesson 01).
This is the head trader's own teaching, verbatim. Use it as the authoritative source for what the academy teaches.

What's up? Welcome to the Academy. Let's start right at the beginning. What trading actually is? In plain, simple terms. No jargon, no fluff, just the real foundation. Let's get into it. Alright, what is trading? Here's the simple definition. Trading is the active buying and selling of financial instruments. That means stocks, indices, commodities, currencies, anything with a price that moves. And the goal? To profit from those price movements. Short term or long term, up or down. That's it. That's the whole game. Sounds almost too simple, right? But inside that one sentence hides everything that separates the 5 % who win from the 95 % who lose. Look at the definition card here. Five points that matter. Point one. You're buying and selling financial instruments. You never need to own a company or ship a product. You trade the price. Point two. Your one and only goal is profiting from the movement itself. Point three. And this one is huge. Trading is not investing. Investors buy and hold for years and hope. Traders exploit movements that happen today, this week, this month. Point four. You can trade long and short. Long means you profit when price rises. Short means you profit when it falls. Most people never learn you can make money in a crashing market. And point five. Markets move every single day, which means opportunity every single day. So why did I personally choose trading? Three reasons. Freedom, scalability and performance. Freedom first. Trading is completely independent of location and time. Laptop, connection, done. Scalability. No employees, no inventory, no customer support. A 10 -lot trade takes the same click as a one -lot trade. Your business grows without growing your workload. And performance. Only results count, not hours. If you're good, the market pays you directly. So here's the honest summary. Trading gives you maximum freedom, but it demands full responsibility. No boss to blame, no team to carry you. Your decisions, your outcome. And honestly, that's exactly the point. Now let's make it concrete. Trading versus a classic business. Trading. Minimal starting costs. No employees. No products. No office. Works from anywhere in the world. Now the classic company. High fixed costs every month. Employees to manage. Customers to keep happy. Bureaucracy everywhere. And years of buildup before real profit. The entry barriers live on completely different planets. Stack all of that together, and you land on one sentence. Trading is one of the most capital efficient business models in the world. Minimal capital, zero infrastructure, unlimited upside. That's why you're here. Next lesson? Why most traders still lose, and what the winners do differently. See you there.$kb$)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, lang = EXCLUDED.lang,
  content = EXCLUDED.content, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO mentor_knowledge (slug, title, lesson_id, lang, sort, content)
VALUES ('what-is-trading-de', $kb$Was ist Trading? (Lektion 01, DE)$kb$, 'l1', 'de', 100, $kb$Transcript of the academy lesson video: Was ist Trading? (Lektion 01, DE).
This is the head trader's own teaching, verbatim. Use it as the authoritative source for what the academy teaches.

In diesem Video gucken wir uns einmal an, was ist Trading und das ist so ein bisschen die Grundbasis von allem, damit du verstehst, was die Hintergründe sind, was überhaupt möglich ist. Wir gehen Themen durch wie zum Beispiel, warum ich mich persönlich für Trading entschieden habe vor fünf Jahren und was so die Unterschiede zwischen einem klassischen Unternehmen sind und das gucken wir uns jetzt einfach direkt mal an. Ja, was ist Trading überhaupt? Und da habe ich einfach mal eine Definition rausgeschrieben, Trading ist der aktive Kauf und Verkauf von Finanzinstrumenten. Bedeutet, wir können von steigenden Kursen, als auch von fallenden Kursen profitieren, was das Ganze enorm, enorm attraktiv macht. Warum? Weil wir beim Investieren theoretisch gesehen nur kaufen können. Ja, wenn wir in Aktien investieren, können wir nur kaufen, wir können nicht verkaufen, das heißt, wir profitieren beim Investieren nur von steigenden Kursen. Beim Trading profitieren wir von egal, welcher Marktsituation. Und das ist das Schöne daran, ja. Wir können in Märkten wie dem Aktienmarkt, den Indices, Indices sind zum Beispiel Sachen wie der DAX, der S &P 500, der Naster Act, US 30, vielleicht eins von denen schon mal gehört, Rohstoffe wie Gold, Silber, Kupfer, Öl oder eben Währungen wie der Euro, Great Britain Pound, der US Dollar, in all diesen Märkten können wir trading, ja. Und das Ziel am Ende des Tages ist es, von kurzfristigen oder eben langfristigen Kursbewegungen zu profitieren. So, zusammengefasst, wir können kaufen und verkaufen, wir kaufen oder verkaufen Finanzinstrumente. Ja, das Ziel ist es, von Kursbewegungen zu profitieren, entweder eben von langfristigen über Wochen hinweg oder von kurzfristigen, von paar Stunden, manchmal Tagen, manchmal Minuten. Trading ist nicht investieren, der Unterschied ist eben, wir können zwei Richtungen trading, einmal long gehen, long es kaufen und einmal short gehen, short es verkaufen, ja. Und noch ein Riesenvorteil ist eben, der Markt bewegt sich jeden Tag. Also so gut wie jeden Tag haben wir Möglichkeiten und haben die Möglichkeit, Brot nach Hause zu bringen, ja, Geld zu verdienen. Ja, warum habe ich mich für Trading entschieden? Da gibt es drei primäre Punkte, das ist einmal die Freiheit, die Skalierbarkeit, was langfristig ein Riesenthema ist und die Leistung, ja, das nur deine Leistung zählt. Freiheit, na ja, relativ simpel, wir sindortsunabhängig, wir sind zeitunabhängig, wir können trading, wann wir wollen, wir können trading, wo wir wollen, ja. Skalierbarkeit, wir haben keine Mitarbeiter, wir haben kein Lager, wir haben keine Kundenbetreuung. Bedeutet, wir sind von niemandem abhängig und das ist das Schöne daran. Und nur die Leistung zählt, ja, bei einem Unternehmen ist es meistens so, je mehr Stunden du reinsteckst, je mehr du arbeitest, desto profitabler wird dein Business. Im Trading ist es nicht so. Im Trading wirst du nicht für Arbeitsstunden bezahlt. Ich würde sogar mal in den Raum werfen, je mehr du machst im Trading oder je mehr du tradest, aktiv tradest, desto schlechter wird die Performance. Wir brauchen effektiv ein bis drei Stunden am Tag, wo wir trading mehr ist, nicht nötig. Das heißt im Trading mehr ist nicht gleich mehr, anders wie bei ganz vielen anderen Businessmodellen, ja. Das heißt Trading bietet maximale Freiheit, weil eine größere Freiheit kannst du nicht erzielen bei voller Eigenverantwortung. Nur du bist für deinen Erfolg verantwortlich, niemand sonst. Du bist von niemandem abhängig und das ist das Schöne daran. Und das merken wir, wenn wir einmal die Vorteile gegenüber dem klassischen Geschäftsmodell durchgehen. Die Vorteile von Trading sind wir eben schon durchgegangen, ja, wir haben geringe Startkosten, wir haben keine Mitarbeiter, keine Produkte, kein Büro und wir sind eben frei weltweit möglich. Gegenüber einem klassischen Unternehmen, wir haben hohe Fixkosten. Ein klassisches Unternehmen hat meistens ein Büro, hat meistens Lagerkosten und extrem viele laufende Fixkosten, als auch Startkosten. Selbst die Startkosten für ein Unternehmen sind relativ hoch. Mitarbeiterführung, Leadership, das ist eine der größten Punkte, warum Unternehmen scheitern, weil die Unternehmer ihr Team nicht richtig führen können. Riesenthema, extrem, extrem schwierig sich beizubringen. Brauchen wir nicht im Trading, warum? Naja, du musst dich mastern, niemanden sonst. Kundenmanagement, wir haben auch keine Kunden im Trading. Bürokratie ist in gewisser Art und Weise vorhanden, aber nicht mal ansatzweise so stressig wie in einem Unternehmen. In einem Unternehmen musst du Löhne durchgehen, musst du Deadlines einhalten, musst du Steuern richtig abrechnen. Da geht es um alles, du brauchst so viel. Leute für dieses Thema, für die Bürokratie, die das alles managen, im Trading, super simpel. Ist natürlich vorhanden, wir müssen Steuern zahlen, je nachdem, ob du eben in Deutschland bist oder in einem anderen Land, aber nicht mal ansatzweise so schlimm wie bei einem Unternehmen. Und es geht ewig lange, also die lange Aufbaufase sind bei Unternehmen eben typisch, du hast eine sehr, sehr lange Phase, um so ein Unternehmen aufzubauen, je nachdem, was der Plan ist und wie groß der Plan ist, zwei bis fünf Jahre, bis so ein Unternehmen mal anläuft, bis es mal profitabel wird. Das heißt, zusammengefasst können wir sagen, Trading ist eines der kapitaleffizientesten Geschäftsmodelle überhaupt. Du hast geringe Startkosten, du brauchst nicht Multimillionen, um Trading zu starten. Das reichen ein paar Hunderten, ein paar Tausend Euro, um den Tradingkonto zu eröffnen, um mal zu starten. Du hast Freiheit auf einem ganz anderen Niveau, weil als Unternehmer kannst du auch ortsunabhängig arbeiten oder zeitunabhängig arbeiten in einer gewissen Atemweise, aber nicht mal ansatzweise so gut wie du es in Trading machen kannst, weil du dauerhaft mit irgendwelchen Sachen beschäftig bist, irgendwelche Sachen managen musst. Im Trading hast du ein bis drei Stunden Fokus pro Tag. That's it.$kb$)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, lang = EXCLUDED.lang,
  content = EXCLUDED.content, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO mentor_knowledge (slug, title, lesson_id, lang, sort, content)
VALUES ('why-90-percent-lose', $kb$Why 90% of Traders Lose (lesson 03)$kb$, 'l3', 'en', 100, $kb$Transcript of the academy lesson video: Why 90% of Traders Lose (lesson 03).
This is the head trader's own teaching, verbatim. Use it as the authoritative source for what the academy teaches.

What's up, traders? Welcome back to the Academy. Today's lesson is the one nobody likes to hear and everybody needs. Why most traders lose money? We'll break down the exact mistakes that empty accounts so you can skip every single one of them. Let's get into it. Welcome back to the Academy. Today's lesson is the one most courses skip because it doesn't sell well. Why most traders lose money? And I want to be completely honest with you here because if you understand why the majority fails, you already know half of what you need to end up on the other side of that statistic. So let's rip the Band-Aid off. Let's start with the numbers because they are brutal. Across brokers and regulators, the data keeps landing in the same range. 80 to 90 percent of retail traders lose money. Think about that. Out of every 10 people who open a trading account, maybe one or two survive long term. Most of them fail within their first one or two years. But here's the part that should actually make you optimistic. When you study how they lose, it's almost never bad luck. It's the same handful of mistakes repeated over and over. And mistakes that repeat in a pattern are mistakes you can avoid. So before we go further, write this one sentence down because it's the foundation for everything that follows. The majority doesn't lose because of the market. The market doesn't hunt beginners. They lose because of their own decisions. Decisions made without a plan, without rules and under emotion. Now let's get specific. When you break down thousands of blown accounts, the same five patterns show up again and again. I call them the five account destroyers. And as we go through them, be honest with yourself, because I promise you'll recognize at least one of them. Destroyer number one, trading without a plan. No written rules, no defined entry, no defined exit. Every single decision comes straight from gut feeling. And here's the problem with gut feeling in the market. Your gut was trained for survival, not for trading. It buys when things feel safe, which is usually the top. And it panics when things feel scary, which is usually the bottom. Destroyer number two, over trading. Taking 15 trades a day when there were maybe two good setups. Forcing entries out of boredom, just to feel like you're doing something. Every unnecessary trade costs you twice. Once in fees and spread and once in mental energy. The best traders I know are bored most of the day. That's not a bug, that's the job. Destroyer number three, risk that is far too high. Position sizes where one single trade decides the whole week. Maybe you know this feeling. One loss and suddenly you need three winners just to get back to zero. That's how accounts die, not slowly, but in two or three oversized trades. An account can survive many small mistakes. It cannot survive one big one. Destroyer number four, unrealistic expectations, zero patience and the endless hunt for the holy grail. That one secret indicator, that one strategy with 95% win rate. Let me save you years, it does not exist. Every profitable trader you admire is running a system with losing trades in it. The difference is, their system survives the losers. And Destroyer number five, strategy hopping. Trying a strategy for one week, taking three losses and jumping to the next YouTube video. You never give any approach enough time to prove itself, so you never collect real data about anything. It's like digging 10 wells two meters deep, instead of one well 20 meters deep. You do a lot of work and never hit water. Put all five together and you get the simple truth. Beginners lose money because they trade without a system. But there's a layer underneath those five mistakes. And it's the real engine behind all of them. Emotions. Three of them do most of the damage. Fear makes you close winning trades way too early. You take 20 pips of a 200 pip move just to feel safe. And it makes you skip perfectly good setups after two losses in a row. Greed does the opposite. It whispers double the size. This one is certain. And suddenly you're breaking your own rules on the most obvious trade of the week. And hope? Hope is the most expensive one. Hope lets losses run because it will come back. Hope holds a bias for days after the market has already proven it wrong. And these three don't act alone. They feed a loop. Watch the chain. Emotions lead to bad decisions. Bad decisions lead to broken rules. Broken rules lead to losses. And every loss produces even more emotion. More fear. More greed. More hope. Round and round it goes. And with every lap the account gets smaller and the decisions get worse. This loop right here is what actually empties trading accounts. Not the market. The loop. On top of all that, there are four quiet account killers that nobody talks about because they don't feel like mistakes while you're making them. First, information overload. 20 strategies, 50 opinions, 5 gurus who all contradict each other. And you end up trading a random mix of everything and mastering nothing. Second, lack of real practice. Watching 100 hours of videos feels like progress, but knowledge is not skill. Nobody learns to swim from a book. Third, no feedback. You make the same mistake for six months and never notice because nobody is looking over your shoulder and your journal is empty. And fourth, marketing instead of reality. Lambos, beach clips, 10,000 a month with one hour a day. That's a sales pitch, not a career plan. If you build your expectations on marketing, the real market will feel like a scam. It isn't. It's just real. So let me give you the actual formula, the honest one, without shortcuts. Profitability equals knowledge plus experience plus discipline plus execution. Look at that equation again. Knowledge is only one of the four terms. That's why watching more videos alone never made anyone profitable. Experience you collect in the market. Discipline you build by following rules when it hurts. And execution is doing the right thing on a Tuesday afternoon when nobody is watching. The good news? Every single term is trainable. So here's the bottom line of this lesson. And it's the mindset shift that separates the 10% from the 90. Successful traders focus on the process, not on a single trade. One trade means nothing. It's noise. A hundred trades executed by the same clean system. That's a business. Build the system. Follow the system. Review the system. Do that and the results stop being luck and start being math. In the next lesson, we begin building exactly that system. Starting with the question, what is retail money? And why does the market know exactly where it sits? See you there.$kb$)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, lang = EXCLUDED.lang,
  content = EXCLUDED.content, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO mentor_knowledge (slug, title, lesson_id, lang, sort, content)
VALUES ('retail-money', $kb$What Is Retail Money? (lesson 08)$kb$, 'l8', 'en', 100, $kb$Transcript of the academy lesson video: What Is Retail Money? (lesson 08).
This is the head trader's own teaching, verbatim. Use it as the authoritative source for what the academy teaches.

Welcome back. In the last lesson, we saw why most traders lose. Today, we zoom out and look at the battlefield itself because the market is not one big anonymous crowd. It has sides. And today's question decides which side you're on. What exactly is retail money and why does the market treat it the way it does? Let's define it properly. Retail traders are private market participants who trade with their own capital. That's the beginner with his first 500 euros. The hobby trader checking charts on his lunch break. The YouTube trader copying setups from videos. The small investor with a broker app on his phone. Very different people. But look at what they all share. Small to medium capital so they can't move the market. Limited information because nobody sells them the real data feeds. The same education from the same free sources. And therefore, and this is the key point, almost identical strategies. So when professionals say retail money, they don't mean one person. They mean the huge predictable mass of private traders acting in the same way at the same spots. Keep that image in your head, not a person, a mass. Now ask yourself, where did you learn trading? Be honest, because when we look at where this entire mass gets its knowledge, it comes down to exactly two sources and both produce the same result. Now, none of this is wrong. That's the trap. It's all correct enough to feel professional. But notice what just happened. Millions of people. Two sources. One identical toolbox. And that produces a structural problem that has nothing to do with intelligence. Everyone learns the same concepts. So everyone sees the same setups. So everyone enters at the same levels. And everyone places their stop loss at nearly the same obvious spot. Under the same swing low, above the same resistance, always. Think this through with me, step by step, because this chain is the whole lesson. Same education leads to same analysis. Same analysis leads to same positioning. Same positioning means the crowd often trades identically. And now the uncomfortable question. If you can predict where millions of stop losses are sitting, do you think the players with billions in capital can't? The crowd isn't just wrong sometimes, the crowd is visible. Which brings us to the next question. If these classic retail concepts are everywhere, why do they so often fall short? To answer that, let's be very precise about what each tool actually tells you and what it doesn't. Support and resistance shows you where something could happen. A zone. A possibility. Nothing more. RSI shows you what has already happened. It's calculated from past prices. By the time it's oversold, the move is history. And chart patterns, they give you probabilities. A pattern that works 60% of the time still fails four times out of ten. And the pattern never tells you which four. Notice what all three have in common. None of them answer the questions that actually decide you trade. Why is the market moving right now? Who is buying? Who is selling? Where is the liquidity sitting that will stop or fuel this move? Every classic retail tool describes the result of the battle. None of them show you the battle itself. Retail methods show the result. Not the cause. And this is exactly where level two data enters the picture. Here's the difference in one image. A retail trader looks at the market and sees the price and a few indicators drawn on top of the price. An institution looks at the same market and sees the price plus the order book behind it plus level two data showing who is bidding and offering at every level plus the liquidity map of the whole market depth. Same market two completely different amounts of information. It's like playing poker where one side sees only their own cards and the other side sees the odds, the bets and half the tables tells. That gap is not talent. It's data and it can be closed. So here is the shift this whole course is built on and it happened for me personally in exactly this order. Before, RSI, trend lines, classic retail setups, grinding the same concepts as everyone else and wondering why the results were random. Then one day, the insight that changes everything. If the majority uses the same concepts and still loses, why on earth would I keep trading the same way? That question forces the change. Stop staring at the result and start studying the cause. Focus on market participants. Who is actually in this move? Focus on liquidity, where the money is sitting. Focus on level two data, what the order book is really doing. And the result of that change? More understanding, better decisions and an end to flying blind. Not a magic win rate. Clarity. You finally see the same game the big players see. So remember the bottom line of this lesson? Understand the cause, not just the result. That single sentence separates the mass from the professionals. And in the next video, we get practical. We open up level two data itself and I'll show you live what the order flow looks like behind a real chart. See you there.$kb$)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, lang = EXCLUDED.lang,
  content = EXCLUDED.content, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO mentor_knowledge (slug, title, lesson_id, lang, sort, content)
VALUES ('level-1-vs-level-2', $kb$Level 1 vs. Level 2 Data (lesson 09)$kb$, 'l9', 'en', 100, $kb$Transcript of the academy lesson video: Level 1 vs. Level 2 Data (lesson 09).
This is the head trader's own teaching, verbatim. Use it as the authoritative source for what the academy teaches.

Welcome back. In the last lesson, we ended with one sentence. Understand the cause, not just the result. Today, we make that sentence practical, because this lesson answers the question the entire rest of this course is built on. What exactly is level two data? And I promise you, once you see markets this way, you can't unsee it. Let's start with an honest inventory. Open any beginner's charting screen, and you'll find the same four things. Candles, support zones, resistance zones, and a couple of indicators blinking underneath. Now here's the reality check nobody gives you. All of that lives on the chart, and the chart is a history book. It only shows you the end result of a fight between buyers and sellers that is already over. Reading only the chart is like watching a football match, but only ever seeing the final score. Let me make that concrete with a single candle. Take one strong bullish candle, big green body, closing near its high. The naive reading says the price went up, technically true, and completely useless. The real reading says, during those minutes, buyers were more aggressive than sellers. They lifted every offer in their way and kept pushing. Same candle, same picture, but the second reading tells you about the people behind the move, not just the outcome. So burn this into your memory, because it's the sentence that separates chart readers from market readers. The chart shows you what happened. It never, ever shows you why it happened. To find the why, we go all the way down to the foundation. Every market on this planet, stocks, forex, crypto, oranges at a street market, runs on exactly one mechanism, supply and demand. That's it. Everything else is decoration. And that mechanism only knows three states. State one, buyers dominate, demand eats through supply and the price rises. State two, balance. Buyers and sellers are evenly matched and the market chops sideways. State three, sellers dominate. Supply overwhelms demand and the price falls. Look at any chart in your life again. Every single day on it is just these three states taking turns. Nothing more. Now think about what that really means. If price only moves because of buying and selling pressure, then indicators don't move markets. Chart patterns don't move markets. Trendlines have never moved a single tick. Markets move because of four real things. Buy orders hitting the book, sell orders hitting the book, liquidity sitting at levels, and large participants pushing serious size. Everything else is commentary about these four. Why does this matter for your trading? Because it changes the questions you ask. Most traders only ever ask one question. Where is the price? That's like a detective asking only where the body is. Professionals ask three more questions. Why is the price there? Who is moving it right now? And where is the liquidity sitting that will either stop this move or fuel it? Those three questions are the entire job. And the core of this lesson in one line, markets move on orders and liquidity, not on indicators. And here's the good news. The tool that makes those orders visible exists. And it's exactly what this course teaches. It's called level two data, the order flow behind the chart. Enough theory, let me show you what it actually looks like live. What you're seeing right now is a live order flow chart, the same market you know, but with the curtain pulled back. Every bubble is real executed volume. Cyan bubbles are aggressive buyers hitting the market. Magenta bubbles are aggressive sellers. And the size of the bubble is the size of the money behind it. Small dots are retail noise. Big bubbles are serious players. Now watch what happens around the big bubbles. Price reacts exactly where they cluster. That's liquidity in action. The real cause behind every move you've ever stared at on a naked chart. This is the difference between watching the final score and watching the actual match. And from the next lesson on, we'll learn to read this step by step, signal by signal. See you there.$kb$)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title = EXCLUDED.title, lesson_id = EXCLUDED.lesson_id, lang = EXCLUDED.lang,
  content = EXCLUDED.content, updated_at = now();

INSERT INTO mentor_knowledge (slug, title, lesson_id, lang, sort, content)
VALUES ('volume-profile-footprint', $kb$Volume Profile, HVN/LVN & Footprint (lesson 11/12)$kb$, 'l11', 'en', 100, $kb$Transcript of the academy lesson video: Volume Profile, HVN/LVN & Footprint (lesson 11/12).
This is the head trader's own teaching, verbatim. Use it as the authoritative source for what the academy teaches.

Welcome back. Over the last lessons, we broke down Level 2 data and the volume profile, piece by piece. Now we put it all together and see how they work in combination live in the market. There are two ways to trade this. The first is trading view. The platform 99 % of traders used to analyze. On trading view, our access to real Level 2 data is limited. But for the beginning, it's more than enough to take a real step in the right direction. The second way is professional software connected directly to an exchange with real, reliable, long -term Level 2 data. We'll look at both. We start on trading view. And then we move into the software with all its features. So this is trading view. We're almost every trader analyzes the market. Let me show you this setup. On the left is my favorite bar. Every drawing tool I actually use lives here. Up at the top, we have an add -on. The volume profile, fixed range. This lets us read the traded volume of any range we select right here on trading view. So we get a clear view of where volume actually happened. And we have different views of the market. There's the line chart. There's the candle chart, probably the most popular. And then there's the volume footprint chart where we see the delta volume and the orders themselves inside every candle. For trading view, honestly, that's very good. You can absolutely work with it. But the footprint inside the professional software is another level, more precise, more accurate. Why? Because it pulls data trading view simply doesn't have. Data straight from one of the largest exchanges. There's also the session volume profile. The volume of each single day, which is extremely useful and we'll come back to it. Let's start with the volume profile. Because if you master this one tool, you can already achieve a lot. The market is moved by volume, by orders. So the question is, how we use it? We draw the volume profile from the load to the current point in the market. Not low to high, because that would leave a big gap and distort everything. From that draw, we get three levels. The value area low, the value area high and the point of control. The price where the most volume traded. Now here is the rule, and it's the most important part. We do not trade at the point of control. We wait for the value area low to be swept, taken out. The structure here is bullish. We already broke the high. So we're only looking for longs. Watch closely. Price comes down toward the value area low but at the exact moment of that candle on the one minute time frame, we were not yet at the value area low. So we do not cheat. We do not call it a valid trade. We stay patient and we wait for real confirmation. As new volume comes in, the profile itself keeps changing so we keep redrawing it to stay accurate. Now the value area high of the down move gets taken out. We can drop to the five minute to see it cleanly and from there we simply continue in the direction of the move, a continuation short. Then the value area low of that move gets taken out too and price pushes into a low volume node, an imbalance zone. This is a very strong criterion because price moves fast through areas where almost no volume traded. And notice how precise this has to be. The sweep of the value area low happens only minimally, just barely, exactly like here. That precision waiting for the level to be taken by the smallest margin and not a moment early is the entire edge of this method now for the actual entry. The market starts to consolidate. We draw the value area of that consolidation and now we have two options. We wait for a sustainable break of the resistance we built plus the prior value area low. There it is, a sustainable break landing right between the levels. We take it out, we get a clean pin bar and now everything comes down to what the footprint tells us. Look here, we have a negative delta but a positive candle. The delta tells us how many active orders hit the market negative delta means more aggressive cells than buys. So if cells were dominant, what push the price up? Passive buy orders, they sat in the book, they absorbed all those cells and they lifted the market. That is absorption and absorption is our entry trigger. We enter with a tight stop and within seconds the trade runs into profit, a risk reward of 1 to 2.8 and if we target further up to 1 to 4.6, of course full order flow trading is more than one tool. In the software, we watch three things at once, a heat map, the deep trades and a real footprint and the single most important entry confirmation of them all is absorption. Let me show you exactly what it looks like. A lot of cell orders come into the market but the market does not drop. Those aggressive market cells are being absorbed by passive orders sitting in the order book and the heat map shows us precisely where those passive orders are. Here, 101 cell contracts hit, then just two buys and the candle closes bullish. That is absorption. Now compare that to a healthy move. Buy orders come in and the market goes up. Buys in, price up. It keeps going cleanly until the cellers finally step back in aggressively and take control. Reading that difference, absorbed versus healthy, is what tells us who is really in charge. Now watch a top form in real time. Up here at the ceiling buyers keep getting absorbed. They throw orders at it again and again but they cannot break through. When that absorption happens at a higher time frame point of interest, for example, the value area high of the day, that is your signal to start looking for shorts. Look at the footprint on the right. It's the same area of the market, just a different view of the same orders. Up at the top we see trapped buyers. 214, 202, 290 buy contracts, all trying to break higher and nothing happens. A positive delta of 11%, 11 % more aggressive buyers than sellers and yet the very next candle sells straight off. Those are trapped buyers. So here is the trade. We wait for large cell orders to finally come in and for the prior cell orders to break. Then we short. Stop goes above the absorption and we target the next low or the value area low of the long move. A risk reward of one to one and a half and up to one to six if we target the daily volume profile. One last piece. Why absorption matters so much mechanically? Imagine 500 short contracts are sitting open in the market. Now think carefully about what happens when a short position closes. Whether it closes in profit or in loss, closing a short is an active buy. So when those 500 shorts get closed out up here, they don't just disappear. They act as pure buying power, pushing directly in our favor. That is the deeper reason we want to see absorption in the first place. It reveals that passive orders have quietly entered the market. Orders we otherwise could never see. And it tells us which direction the hidden power is really leaning. And that's the whole method. It's simple. The volume profile plus a few clean entry confirmations that give us real momentum. And here's the beauty of it. These trades are either instant losses where you know within two minutes that you were wrong and you're out. Or their momentum trades with almost zero drawdown. If you already trade a strategy, add the volume profile on top and watch how much it sharpens your edge. And if you're brand new, then you're starting with something that actually works over the long term. These are the exact systems traded by world champions for years. I learned them from the very best and now this knowledge is yours. I'll see you in the next lesson.$kb$)
