INSERT INTO mentor_knowledge (slug, title, lesson_id, lang, sort, content)
VALUES ('signals-howto-en', $kb$How to copy a signal (signals tutorial)$kb$, NULL, 'en', 100, $kb$Transcript of the academy lesson video: How to copy a signal (signals tutorial).
This is the head trader's own teaching, verbatim. Use it as the authoritative source for what the academy teaches.

Hey, so you've joined the Signals channel. In the next three minutes, I'll show you exactly what to do when a signal drops, how to read it, how to place it in your broker app, and how to manage it like a professional. Grab your phone, let's do this together. First, let's read the signal. Every signal has the same anatomy, the direction and the instrument. Here, it's a cell on gold. The entry price, where the trade starts, the stop loss, your protection. This is where the trade is simply wrong. And four take profit levels, where we lock in profits step by step. And the golden rule right in the message, one to 3% risk per trade, maximum, no exceptions. Step two, open your broker app and find the instrument from the signal. Tap the symbol, open the chart, and take three seconds to check where the price is compared to the entry. If it's already way past the entry, you let this one go. There's always a next signal. Step three, the order. We tap sell, and now the most important field of all, the lot size. This is where your one to 3% rule becomes a number. Small account? Start with 0.01, a micro lot. The size is calculated so that if the stop loss gets hit, you lose one to 3% of your account, not more. Step four, type in the stop loss and take profit. Exactly the numbers from the signal. Not your feeling, not roughly, exactly. The stop loss is not optional. It goes in before you confirm the trade. A position without a stop loss is not a trade. It's a hope with a deposit. Now we confirm and check the chart. You should see your position line, the red stop loss line above, and the take profit line below. If those lines are on the chart, you're done. No staring, no refreshing every minute. The trade manages itself now. And when take profit one gets hit, the channel posts it, and this is where professionals cash flow. Close a part of the position at TP1, move on to TP2 with the rest. And the one thing you never do? You never, ever move your stop loss further away. That single habit is what separates accounts that grow from accounts that vanish. That's the whole workflow. Read the signal, check the entry, size by risk, set stop and targets, confirm and let it play out. Simple, repeatable, boring. And that's exactly why it works. See you in the channel.$kb$)
